      "writtenOn": "2021-10-21T09:03:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I really don\u0027t know, I\u0027m not an expert in MM / arm nor arm64 el2 functionality.\n\nBut what you\u0027re trying to do -at a high level- seems fundamentally reasonable.\nPresumably it is to make use of some better non-exec/read/write\nprotection mechanism - maybe it\u0027s per priv level?\nNot clear to me why bpf is special though...  I\u0027d guess it\u0027s the jit, but why?\n\nPart of the problem is all I see are callsites, and not what the hooks\nactually do.\nI assume that it\u0027s simply a matter of toggling some memory protection magic.\nBut is this hw specific? Special cpu instructions? Do these become\nno-ops on older hardware?\nDoes it need indirect calls into functions provided by some hw-specific module?\nIs it trivial? fast? complex? slow?\nI\u0027d suggest showing a patch that isn\u0027t just hooks, but does indeed\ninclude the implementation of these hooks...\nor explaining what the hooks *do* or need to do.\n\nFurthermore, I\u0027d suggest getting in touch with the upstream bpf\ndevelopers and/or mm folks and/or arm people, and describing the\nproblem and working something out.\n\nMy gut feeling is tracepoints are for debugging and not for\nimplementing functionality, so I\u0027m not a fan of 2a - but I may be\nmisunderstanding the suggestion.\nThis leaves 2b, or some approach like 2b - with explicit call sites to\ndo *something* (but what?) in the right spots in the bpf alloc/free\npaths.\n\nI don\u0027t understand point [1].\n\nOn Thu, Oct 21, 2021 at 1:01 AM Chinwen Chang (Gerrit)\n\u003cnoreply-gerritcodereview-b-u8KGyaOxooKTntgUg3yA\u003d\u003d@google.com\u003e wrote:",
      "tag": "mailMessageId\u003d\u003cCANP3RGf-tEeZAi2FDOYEJtuXNJwS-90OTXx390cJS8cE8E8taA@mail.gmail.com\u003e",
